ss yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>George Pickens</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 WAS concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58), and DAL funnel 24% of targets his way. A rec-yards spot.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Stefon Diggs</strong> (WR2) \u2014 DAL leak 43 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Jake Ferguson</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 WAS are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.35 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>George Pickens</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 WAS surrender 0.51 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p><strong>Terry McLaurin</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 DAL surrender 0.59 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone DAL throw it (58% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>George Pickens</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to DAL \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Javonte Williams</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>DAL \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>DAL are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Javonte Williams</strong> (12 carries, 51 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though DAL's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>George Pickens</strong> projects for <strong>5 targets and 52 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Jake Ferguson</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 23 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script